\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Colombia \highlight\uc2  Peso/Amplifier -2:  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2 : Has  \highlight22\uc2 Intervened \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 342 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 September 1997\par 23:10\par \uc2  Emerging Markets Report \par \uc2 EM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1997, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Ocampo, who as finance minister also presides over the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  board of directors, said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and government have sold about $200 million on Colombia's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market over the last two weeks to slow the peso's slide. \par \par \uc2  The peso's weighted average exchange rate on Wednesday was 1,251.68 to the dollar, a decline of 3.7% from Monday. The nominal depreciation is about 25% from the beginning of the year. \par \par \uc2  Both dollar traders and Bogota stock brokers said they saw foreign investment funds Monday and Tuesday pull their money out of Colombia as a result of the violence and the ensuing weakening of the peso. \par \par \uc2  "There's kind of a snowball effect," one trader said. \par \par \uc2  But Ocampo insisted the decline in the peso is the result of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's efforts to lower interest rates. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  three weeks ago lowered several key interest rates by one-half of one percentage point, a policy that makes peso deposits less attractive. \par \par \uc2  Traders agree that interest rates are low for the peso, prompting investors to save in other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Seasonal and technical factors have also contributed to the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's slip, including expectations of increased declines in the peso, which have encouraged companies to pre-pay dollar-denominated obligations and to sell dollars forward instead of into the spot market. \par \par \uc2  "They fear the peso will continue to fall," said ING-Barings' Garcia. \par \par \uc2  Also, traders said many banks had assumed short positions in futures \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 , with the expectation that the peso would strengthen as the year progresses. \par \par \uc2  When that didn't happen, many banks were forced to buy dollars to prevent further losses. \par \par \uc2  "They decided to take their losses now (by buying dollars)," one \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader said. \par \par \uc2  The peso appreciated 15-17% against the dollar in real terms in 1996. Nominal depreciation was about 1.8% in 1996 against a local inflation rate of 21.6%. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) DOW JONES EMERGING MARKETS REPORT  10-09-97 \par \par \uc2  2110GMT \par \par \uc2  1192 1802 \par \par \uc2 Dokument em00000020011006dt9a00ctr\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
