\par\par \b \uc2  Political Crisis Sparks Renewed Slide Of Colombian Peso \b0\par\par\uc2 144 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 August 1995\par 23:01\par \uc2  Emerging Markets Report \par \uc2 EM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1995, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  BOGOTA (DJ-EMR)--The Colombian peso continued its slide against the dollar amid heavy trading Friday despite repeated efforts by the Colombian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to shore up its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , a trader said. \par \par \uc2  The peso closed at 973.51, and was weighted down by a continuing political crisis and a growing trade deficit. The closing price was at the top of the exchange band maintained by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the point at which it sells all the dollars the market demands. \par \par \uc2  "Despite almost permanent \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market, the Banco de la Republica (\b \highlight22\uc2 Colombia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) could not reverse nor stop the tendency," one trader said. \par \par \uc2  Friday's trading volume was $216 million, more than double the normal daily trading average of about $100 million. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) DOW JONES EMERGING MARKETS REPORT 08-25-95 \par \par \uc2  1701EDT \par \par \uc2  1802 \par \par \uc2 Dokument em00000020011025dr8p00acm\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
